contentText:
For La Reezy, the lyrical mutant birthed in New Orleans, where the spirits of Lil Wayne and YoungBoy run laps around the city, he’s slowly embracing his stardom while remembering the person at the center of it all: Khayree.

“My Zoom name is Khayree, and I didn&#8217;t even realize that,” he says days before embarking on a multi-city tour with Chance the Rapper. “I&#8217;m still thrifting. I&#8217;m still bike-riding. I&#8217;m still enjoying myself and allowing the music to be a vessel of what Khayree is thinking about. I get imposter syndrome the most when I&#8217;m not being human enough. So it&#8217;s still keeping Khayree alive.”

Despite his superhuman wordplay, La Reezy, you can say, is having his Spider-Man moment. With praise from Kendrick Lamar, J. Cole and Tyler, the Creator, Reezy had the holy trinity of rap co-signs before he was even old enough to legally drink. Mixtapes such as Welcome&nbsp; La Reezyana Vol. 1 &nbsp;showcased his promise and dexterity, while his debut album,&nbsp; Skiddle Bandana , prided itself on colorful storytelling, charisma and the unwavering charm of a young Black man growing up in real time.

With Kendrick Lamar’s&nbsp; Section.80 &nbsp;in mind, Reezy scribbled down everything he saw from the steps of his mother’s house and gave fans a front-row seat to New Orleans. “Melanate It” is a flavor bomb where Black pride reigns supreme, while “Family Bizzy” is an ode to his relatives and how they shaped his beginnings.

“I know I don&#8217;t have it all figured out,” he acknowledges. “I&#8217;m not the smartest person in the world. I&#8217;m wise, but I still have a lot to learn. If you can see my potential and that I will be great – and I will continue to be great – invest in what I&#8217;ll become.”

Billboard&nbsp; chatted with La Reezy about how Section.80 influenced his debut album&nbsp; Skiddle Bandana, &nbsp;imposter syndrome, family life, touring with Chance the Rapper and more.&nbsp;

Lil Wayne and YoungBoy are considered two of the biggest stars to come out of Louisiana. Who are some of the state’s dark horses that inspired you?

I&#8217;m gonna say Neno Calvin, Rob 49 and recently Mac Phipps. Those are the main ones I can say right now.&nbsp;

Macc played a role on your debut album, too, right?

Yup. Macc is on the title track Skiddle Bandana . The reason I say Macc for context is he&#8217;s part of No Limit [Records] and he was like the lyrical conscious rapper in the group. Then, he got arrested over some lyrics and was incarcerated for over 20 years. He&#8217;s still a very genuine, positive, forward-thinking, supportive guy. To hear that there was a version of what I do back in the day, but wasn&#8217;t able to evolve due to the system –&nbsp; from the pigs – is [crazy]. Macc&#8217;s super supportive and we really resemble each other a lot.&nbsp;

Do you think storytelling and lyricism still carry the same weight with your generation? Where do you think you fit within that conversation?

The world is an evolution of stories told and the words that stick in time the longest are stories – the phrases in a story. Think about the bible, the Quran. Christopher Nolan just put out The Odyssey – that&#8217;s a story. Stories are just artistic ways to depict the world and how we see it today. Some people decide to be intentional with their stories and are disciplined in getting their thoughts out. Some people don&#8217;t. I play the median. It&#8217;s necessary to have it, but, sometimes, it might not be a deep story. It might be a culmination of different words put together and you make this story. For me though, it&#8217;s necessary that I just keep forward-thinking substance at the forefront.&nbsp;

Were there any debut albums that you studied or kept in mind while creating Skiddle Bandana ?

Section.80 . That&#8217;s it. That year seeing Kendrick Lamar at his highest, I&#8217;m always like, &#8220;How did he get here?&#8221; So I&#8217;m always diving back and paying attention.&nbsp;

From the Kendrick Lamar EP to Section.80, how did the love songs evolve? How did the hype songs evolve? What makes this such a thing? How was this received when it was released? Watching the live streams when the album was released [and] really searching the web to see what was going on around that time. Paying attention to the videos like, &#8220;Oh. He probably had some bread, but he didn&#8217;t have a super-big budget.&#8221; I can relate to that. [Making the music], but leaving room to grow and still making it sweet on the ears.

&#8220;Hol&#8217; Up&#8221; is an amazing song, and they said they recorded Section.80 on a broken microphone. I&#8217;m recording these songs in my mama house. I think that album was the inspiration, but doing it in my own way and being intentional with how it&#8217;ll be received down the line.

Your family plays a major role in your life, especially as you highlight on “Family Bizzy.” As your career continues to grow, what responsibility do you feel toward them?

Still being myself. Still being the young kid that would just dance and have fun. Truthfully, everybody has their own life. I could have $100,000 and give it to a family member, but you might get more joy out of finding your passion. I think I&#8217;m just supposed to be myself and be me. It ain&#8217;t really no pressured role because it wasn&#8217;t a pressured role at eight years old.

My mom told me, &#8220;You make me very proud.&#8221; If you make your mom proud then that should be enough. If you make her proud, then you&#8217;re doing it because what more would you need to do if you&#8217;re a product of love? You&#8217;re doing something. That&#8217;s all you need.&nbsp;

“Normal Struggles” speaks to the everyday battles people deal with. What’s one struggle that success or newfound attention hasn’t necessarily made easier for you?&nbsp;

Imposter Syndrome. It can get bad. You just really be in your own mind and sometimes, you&#8217;ll be in your own way. I think music and creation is my way of knowing where I&#8217;m at in life. Sometimes, you don&#8217;t have that. So it&#8217;s still having an existence outside of La Reezy.&nbsp;

You&#8217;re on tour with Chance the Rapper. What are you hoping to show new audiences about La Reezy, and what are you hoping to learn from Chance as a performer?

I wanna learn how Chance manages to keep the vocals warm. That&#8217;s a huge thing that I&#8217;m learning right now and then I don&#8217;t know what I wanna learn. I just wanna learn. I don&#8217;t wanna have an expectation. I just wanna watch and study.

From the fans, [I wanna show them that] hip-hop is still alive. That notion is curated from people that don&#8217;t understand the artform and it&#8217;s paid campaigns when people say that. I just wanna show them that it&#8217;s real music and I&#8217;m here. I want them to invest in the potential.

You’ve gotten co-signs from Cole, Kendrick and Tyler. Which one caught you most off guard, and why has that moment stayed with you?

Kendrick Lamar. To be at the BET Awards and I didn&#8217;t know that he&#8217;d be there and then I just got the balls to go and say what&#8217;s up. I did it with the intent that in 10 years I&#8217;ll meet him and hopefully tell him that I met him. For him to know me around that time while I was putting out my mixtapes like Welcome to La Reezyana Vol. 1 a nd stuff like that [was crazy].

When I met him, I was making Skiddle Bandana and studying Section.80 . That was the ultimate confirmation. All of it&#8217;s been lit. Tyler, The Creator inspired me to hone in on the skill more and dial in on the craft. Care about my art. Anything from the people, I&#8217;m just grateful.&nbsp;

There are already a lot of heavyweight names attached to your story. What do you think people still misunderstand about La Reezy as an artist?

